# The vendor camera provider is not started on this device.
#
# Its legacy passthrough crashes the whole camera stack in a loop. cameraserver
# asks the provider to notify a device state change, the call reaches
# CameraModule::notifyDeviceStateChange in the legacy shim, and that
# dereferences an mModule left null because the HAL underneath never
# initialises:
#
#   F/libc : Fatal signal 11 (SIGSEGV), fault addr 0x8
#            CameraModule::notifyDeviceStateChange
#            (android.hardware.camera.provider@2.4-legacy.so)
#
# The provider dies, cameraserver gets DEAD_OBJECT, the HIDL
# return_status::assertOk() fails and it aborts as well. init restarts both,
# so the pair repeats about once per second: 26 crashes per 25 s measured on
# the phone, on a device with 814 MB of RAM, which also floods the logcat and
# destroys the early-boot evidence.
#
# Not starting the service stops the loop. Measured on the phone: crashes go
# to 0, cameraserver stays up and stable, dumpsys media.camera answers
# instead of reporting "Can't find service", and roughly 30 MB of RAM come
# back.
#
# The camera still enumerates zero devices, since the HAL under it never finds
# any sensor. This only removes the crash loop.
#
# This replaces the copy that ships in the vendor blobs. It is placed after
# every inherit-product in this file so that it wins the make ordering.
service vendor.camera-provider-2-5 /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.camera.provider@2.5-service
#   interface android.hardware.camera.provider@2.5::ICameraProvider legacy/0
#   interface android.hardware.camera.provider@2.4::ICameraProvider legacy/0
#   class hal
    user cameraserver
    group audio camera input drmrpc
    ioprio rt 4
    capabilities SYS_NICE
    task_profiles CameraServiceCapacity MaxPerformance
    disabled
